    /* device_del(&ctlr->dev) is missing here */
</text>
Reason: After `device_add(&ctlr->dev)` succeeds, if `spi_alloc_pcpu_stats` fails, the error handling jumps to `destroy_queue` which only calls `spi_destroy_queue`. Because `device_del` is not called, the controller device remains registered in the driver core. When the function returns, subsequent cleanup frees driver resources (e.g., clocks, queues), leading to use-after-free and unclocked register accesses.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
if (status)
    goto del_ctrl;  /* Changed from direct device_del + goto free_bus_id */
...
destroy_queue:
    spi_destroy_queue(ctlr);
del_ctrl:
    device_del(&ctlr->dev);
free_bus_id:
    ...
</text>
Reason: The fix adds a new label `del_ctrl` after `destroy_queue` that calls `device_del`. The queue initialization failure now branches to this label instead of duplicating `device_del`. When per-cpu statistics allocation fails, execution falls through from `destroy_queue` to `del_ctrl`, ensuring the device is properly deregistered from the driver core. This prevents use-after-free and unclocked register accesses by releasing the device registration before the function returns with an error.

